            "<strong>Biostatistics</strong> is the science of collecting, organising, summarising, presenting and interpreting data about living things — in nursing, data about patients, nurses, communities and the care between them — and of reaching conclusions in the presence of variation and uncertainty. Every nurse already uses it: the ward that reports its average length of stay, the matron who says that 68 per cent of the staff attended the training, the midwife who knows that a birth weight of 2.3 kg is below the cut-off, the public health nurse who plots the monthly malaria cases. Its two halves are <strong>descriptive statistics</strong>, which summarise the data that were actually collected — this sample's mean age, this ward's proportion of pressure ulcers — and <strong>inferential statistics</strong>, which use the sample to draw conclusions about the population it came from, with a stated degree of confidence; this module is descriptive, and module 6 is inferential. The distinction rests on two words: a <strong>parameter</strong> is a value that describes the whole population (the true mean haemoglobin of all pregnant women in the Northern Region), written in Greek letters (μ for the mean, σ for the standard deviation) and almost never known; a <strong>statistic</strong> is the corresponding value calculated from a sample (the mean haemoglobin of the 400 women measured), written in Roman letters (x̄, s), always known and always an estimate. Statistics is the discipline of saying how good the estimate is.",
