            "A <strong>count</strong> — 200 maternal deaths, 1,340 malaria cases, 17 needle-stick injuries — is where every health statistic starts and where most misunderstandings start too, because a count means nothing until the reader knows how many people it came from, over how long, and who was left out. Three constructions give the count its meaning. A <strong>ratio</strong> divides one quantity by another that need not contain it — 3 nurses to 1 doctor; a male-to-female ratio of 0.8; the maternal mortality <em>ratio</em>, which divides maternal deaths by live births even though a death and a birth are different things. A <strong>proportion</strong> divides a part by the whole that contains it, so that it lies between 0 and 1 and is usually expressed as a percentage — 28 of 40 nurses are women, a proportion of 0.70 or 70 per cent; prevalence is a proportion. A <strong>rate</strong> measures how fast something happens: events divided by the population at risk <em>over a stated period</em>, multiplied by a convenient constant — per 100, per 1,000, per 100,000 — so that a district of 60,000 and a region of 3 million can be compared on the same scale. Hospital A reports 200 maternal deaths and Hospital B reports 20; Hospital A delivers 40,000 women a year and Hospital B 500; A's ratio is 500 per 100,000 live births and B's is 4,000 — eight times worse, from one-tenth the count. Before believing any figure, the nurse asks three questions: out of how many, over what period, and who was left out.",
