            "<strong>Incidence</strong> counts <em>new</em> cases of a condition arising in a population at risk during a stated period, and measures the speed or force of the disease. <strong>Cumulative incidence</strong> (risk) is new cases divided by the population at risk at the start of the period — 30 new pressure ulcers among 600 immobile patients admitted in a year is a cumulative incidence of 5 per cent, or 50 per 1,000 admissions; the <strong>incidence rate</strong> divides new cases by the person-time actually observed, which matters when people enter and leave the population at different times, as in a cohort study. <strong>Prevalence</strong> counts <em>all</em> existing cases, new and old, as a proportion of the population at one moment (<strong>point prevalence</strong>) or at any time during a period (<strong>period prevalence</strong>), and measures the burden — what services must be provided; a ward prevalence survey on a Tuesday morning that finds 12 of 80 patients with a pressure ulcer gives a point prevalence of 15 per cent. The two are linked by duration: <strong>prevalence ≈ incidence × average duration</strong>. A long illness has high prevalence from low incidence (diabetes, epilepsy, chronic kidney disease); a short one has low prevalence however high its incidence (the common cold); and anything that changes duration changes prevalence without touching incidence — antiretroviral therapy keeps people with HIV alive for decades, so Ghana's HIV prevalence can hold steady or rise while new infections fall, and rising prevalence is then a measure of success. The cross-sectional study measures prevalence and the cohort measures incidence (lesson 2.1), which is one more reason the design decides what can be claimed.",
