          alt: "Numbers that describe a community — the denominator decides what the number means. Three rates, three questions: incidence, new cases in a period — is the problem still spreading; prevalence, all existing cases at one moment — how much care must the service provide; case fatality, deaths among those who had it — how deadly is it once you have it. A long illness has high prevalence and low incidence. Attack rate in an outbreak: number who became ill divided by the number who were exposed, as a percentage; worked example: 120 people ate at the funeral, 30 developed diarrhoea, 30 divided by 120 equals 25 per cent; calculate it separately for each food eaten, and the dish with the highest rate is the likely source. Community diagnosis, in order: collect data from register, survey, records, observation; analyse it and rank the problems; agree priorities with the community, not for them; plan with measurable objectives; implement using local structures; evaluate against the objectives you set. Why the routine data misleads: facility records count attenders, not the population; a new clinic raises the count without raising disease; a stock-out lowers the count without lowering disease; those who never reach a facility never appear at all; so a rise in reported cases may be a rise in reporting. Ghana reports through DHIS2 from every district; data nobody uses at district level stops being collected carefully — feed the analysis back to the people who fill the forms.",
          caption: "Incidence asks whether the problem is still spreading, prevalence asks how much care the service must provide, case fatality asks how deadly it is once you have it — and the attack rate by food is the arithmetic that finds the funeral dish. A rise in reported cases may be a rise in reporting.",
