          alt: "The sanitation and water ladder — coverage figures mean nothing until you know which rung is being counted. Sanitation, bottom to top: open defecation, no facility at all; unimproved, open pit or hanging latrine; limited, improved but shared; basic, improved and not shared; safely managed, waste treated too. Community-led total sanitation does not build or subsidise latrines: facilitators walk the community to its own defecation sites and let residents work out where the waste ends up; the community then decides to act, builds with its own materials, and is verified as open-defecation-free at 80 per cent household latrine coverage; ownership is the mechanism, not the slogan. Why communities slide back down: pits and superstructures collapse in the rains, and the poorest households cannot rebuild; coverage falls year on year after verification unless someone keeps returning — declaring is not finishing. The same logic applies to water: a borehole counts as basic only if the round trip to collect water is under thirty minutes; a safe source carried in a dirty bucket and stored uncovered is no longer a safe supply.",
